  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>A forklift carries two duties: a daily pre-use check by the operator under PUWER 1998, and a thorough examination at least every 12 months by a competent person under LOLER 1998</li><li>The pre-use check covers tyres, forks, mast and chains, hydraulics, brakes, steering, horn, lights, seatbelt and controls</li><li>Operators need accredited training such as ITSSAR or RTITB, plus site familiarisation; the check is only as reliable as the operator</li><li>A back-filled paper pad is worthless as evidence; the value is a timestamped record made at the point of observation</li><li>Add your whole team and only pay for who actually works: 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free, which suits a firm with many operators and few office staff</li></ul></div><h2>What a forklift inspection tool is actually for</h2><p>A fork lift truck is provided work equipment, so it sits under the Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998 (PUWER). PUWER requires that work equipment is maintained in a safe condition and inspected so that it stays that way. In practice that means a pre-use check by the operator at the start of each shift or working period.</p><p>Software does not make a forklift safe. A competent operator doing the check properly does. What software does is make the check easier to complete before the shift, harder to skip an item, and faster to get in front of a supervisor. A fault the office learns about at eight in the morning gets the truck fixed. A fault buried in a paper pad in the cab gets someone hurt.</p><h2>The two forklift inspection duties</h2><h3>1. The daily pre-use check (PUWER 1998)</h3><p>The pre-use check is a walk-around plus an operational check, done cold before the truck is used. A typical list covers tyres and wheels, forks and load backrest, mast, chains and hydraulics, brakes and parking brake, steering, horn, lights and beacons, seatbelt or operator restraint, the data plate and rated capacity, and any warning lights or noises on start-up. Faults are logged at the point they are found, not remembered for later.</p><h3>2. The thorough examination (LOLER 1998)</h3><p>Separately, the lifting parts of a forklift fall under the Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment Regulations 1998. LOLER requires a thorough examination by a competent person, at least every 12 months for lifting loads, every 6 months where it lifts people, or in line with a written examination scheme. This is a formal report produced by the competent person, usually an external engineer, and is not something the operator does.</p><h2>The pre-use forklift checklist</h2><ul><li><strong>Tyres and wheels:</strong> damage, wear, cuts, correct pressure, wheel nuts</li><li><strong>Forks and backrest:</strong> heel wear, cracks, bends, the locking pins, the load backrest</li><li><strong>Mast, chains and hydraulics:</strong> chain wear and tension, ram condition, leaks, hoses</li><li><strong>Brakes and steering:</strong> service brake, parking brake, steering response</li><li><strong>Controls and warnings:</strong> horn, lights, beacon, reversing alarm, warning lights on start-up</li><li><strong>Operator restraint:</strong> seatbelt or other restraint in working order</li><li><strong>Data plate:</strong> legible, rated capacity correct for the attachment fitted</li></ul><h2>Training and competence come first</h2><p>The best checklist does nothing if the operator is not competent. Employers must provide adequate training. Most sites train to an accredited standard through bodies such as ITSSAR, RTITB or AITT, followed by job-specific and site familiarisation training, with refreshers to keep skills current. A digital pre-use check supports competence; it does not substitute for it. What it adds is accountability: a timestamp, a named operator, and proof the check was done before the truck moved.</p><h2>What to look for in a forklift inspection tool</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to do the check before the shift, not fill it in afterwards</li><li><strong>Instant fault visibility:</strong> a defect should reach the supervisor in real time, not sit in a book in the cab</li><li><strong>Photo evidence:</strong> a split tyre or bent fork photographed at the point of observation</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes firms with large operator pools. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, operator, truck and every item, for the HSE inspector or insurer who asks later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler runs the daily pre-use check and stores the LOLER certificate against the truck. It does not carry out the LOLER thorough examination or replace the competent person who signs it, and it does not make a truck safe. The competent operator and the competent examiner do that. This page is general information, not legal advice; check current PUWER, LOLER and HSE guidance for your situation. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How often should a forklift be inspected in the UK?</h3><p>There are two duties. A pre-use check should be done by the operator before each shift or working period under PUWER 1998. Separately, a thorough examination by a competent person is required at least every 12 months under LOLER 1998, or every 6 months where the truck lifts people, or in line with a written examination scheme. Confirm the interval that applies to your specific equipment.</p><h3>What is on a forklift pre-use check?</h3><p>A typical pre-use check covers tyres and wheels, forks and load backrest, mast, chains and hydraulics, brakes and parking brake, steering, horn, lights and beacons, seatbelt or restraint, the data plate and rated capacity, and any warning lights or unusual noises on start-up. Adapt the list to your truck types and site.</p><h3>Is a daily forklift check a legal requirement?</h3><p>PUWER 1998 requires work equipment to be maintained and, where appropriate, inspected so that it remains safe to use. A daily pre-use check is the established way employers meet that duty for forklifts, and HSE guidance supports it. This is general information, not legal advice; check current HSE guidance for your situation.</p><h3>Can I run forklift checks over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's pre-use check runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The operator receives each item in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the record generates automatically. A fault flags to the supervisor immediately. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/layered-process-audit-app-uk.html\">Layered process audit app</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/factory-audit-software-uk.html\">Factory audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/machine-safety-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Machine safety inspection checklist</a></li></ul>",
